<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhupatindra Malla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1696">1696</a>–<a href="/years/1722">1722</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhupatindra Malla was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>, one of the three Newar kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. A great builder, he raised the five-storey Nyatapola temple in 1702, the tallest pagoda in the valley, and enlarged the royal palace of fifty-five windows. His statue on a pillar still faces the palace in Bhaktapur's Durbar Square.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhupatindra-malla.jpg" alt="Bhupatindra Malla" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nyeta · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhupatindra_Malla_painting_form_the_18th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bhupatindra Malla was born on 3 November 1674, the son of King Jitamitra Malla and his queen Lālamati. The royal expenditure books record the rites of his boyhood, among them his Chudakarana on 1 March 1685 and his Upanayana on 3 February 1686. He was married to Vishva Lakshmi on 4 November 1687, aged 13, at a feast to which all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> and 16,244 guests from the kingdom's other towns were invited, partly provisioned by a levy in kind called sevākū. As a prince he built a hiti and a public shelter at Thimi.</p><p>Folklore tells of a jealous stepmother who sent him to be killed in a forest, where he was raised by Tibetan craftsmen before returning to claim the throne. Historians reject the story: Jitamitra is known to have had only one queen, and the young prince's rites are recorded at court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>It was long believed that he was crowned in 1696 on his father's death, but a treaty with Yognarendra Malla of Patan five months later still names Jitamitra as king, and a record of around June 1699 shows him performing a ritual. Some historians hold that Jitamitra gave up the throne willingly; others, among them Om Prasad Dhaubhadel, argue that Bhupatindra took it by revolt and kept his father under house arrest. His mother acted as witness to treaties and legal documents, and his uncle Ugra Malla advised him in his early years.</p><p>The Italian missionary Ippolito Desideri described him as the first ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> not to pay tribute to the king of Kantipur. He hunted wild elephants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a>, and in 1715, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and wild animals began to die before the plague of 1716, he offered a bell to the goddess Siddhi Vatsalā.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 15 April 1722; his heir was his son Ranajita Malla, born on 18 November 1702. His reign is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> as its cultural high point, and he is known there as nepaḥ juju, the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newars</a>. Besides the Nyatapola, built in six months, he raised the Simhādhwākhā Lyākū palace, whose window held a glass pane given by a Mughal emperor, and restored the Char Dham replicas. He composed 31 plays, 26 of them in Maithili.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhupatindra Malla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bhupatindra Malla</b>, around 1700, built the Nyatapola temple and enlarged the fifty-five-window palace, and his son Ranajit Malla added the Golden Gate in 1754.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bhaktapur">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</a></li></ul></section>
